  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I checked both by their creation responses; I haven't opened the dashboard in Kibana to see how it renders.\n\n**Data view:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` holds web server access logs. The main fields are:\n- `timestamp`\n- `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as a string)\n- `bytes`\n- `clientip`\n- `request`, `url` and `extension`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `machine.os` and `agent`\n- `host` and `referer`\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-f499da38 Web logs overview\" (id `5a8d4882-1bd1-4352-8d7a-909ac0263b28`) has four panels and defaults to the last 7 days:\n- Total requests\n- Average bytes\n- Unique clients\n- A line chart of requests over time\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-f499da38 5xx spike\" (id `a125325d-14cc-45c7-9365-2cac67a18a63`) is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It counts documents whose `response.keyword` matches `5xx` over the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is above 10. I didn't attach any actions, so it will show alerts in Kibana but won't send notifications anywhere.\n\nThe sample logs are historical, and I didn't shift their timestamps to the present. The rule only counts requests from the last 5 minutes, so it will likely stay quiet unless 5xx requests are indexed in real time.",
